          position: 3,
          name: "Best Whey Protein Isolate",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-whey-protein-isolate/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey isolate better than regular whey protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not for building muscle. At the same dose of protein, isolate and concentrate produce essentially the same results because their amino acid profiles are nearly identical. Isolate is better only in what it leaves out: less lactose, carbohydrate and fat per scoop. That matters if you are lactose sensitive or tracking macros closely, and it is not worth paying extra for otherwise."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey protein isolate lactose-free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most isolates are very low in lactose, typically a fraction of a gram per scoop, but not all are strictly lactose-free, and only some are marketed that way. For most people with lactose intolerance the amount is too small to cause symptoms. If you are highly sensitive, choose a product labeled lactose-free, mix it with water, and consider a lactase tablet as a backup."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I take whey isolate if I am lactose intolerant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes. Research reviewed for a US National Institutes of Health consensus conference found most lactose-intolerant people tolerate around 12 g of lactose at once, far more than an isolate scoop contains. Start with one scoop in water and see how you feel. If you still react, a hydrolyzed isolate or a plant protein are the next options to try."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is hydrolyzed whey isolate worth the extra cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, no. Hydrolyzed whey is absorbed somewhat faster, but studies comparing it with intact whey at matched protein doses have not found a meaningful difference in muscle gained. It often tastes more bitter and costs more. It may be worth trying if regular whey upsets your stomach, but it is not a performance upgrade."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many scoops of whey isolate should I take per day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Enough to close the gap between what you eat and your daily target, which for most people who train is 1.4 to 2.0 g per kilogram of body weight. For many that means one or two scoops. There is no benefit to taking more than you need, and whole foods should still provide most of your protein."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does whey isolate help with weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Indirectly. Protein is more filling than carbohydrate or fat, and higher protein intakes help preserve muscle during a calorie deficit, which is useful when you are dieting. Isolate's low calorie count per gram of protein makes it a convenient tool on a cut. It does not burn fat by itself, and the calorie deficit is still what drives weight loss."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey isolate safe for your kidneys?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In healthy adults, research has not shown that high-protein diets within the ranges recommended for athletes damage the kidneys. People with existing chronic kidney disease are different, because they are often advised to limit protein. If you have kidney disease, diabetes or another condition affecting kidney function, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before using any protein supplement."
