        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run our own lab tests or taste panels for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, published third-party certifications and the research on protein dose and quality, which is how we approach every roundup on the site; our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a> explains the method in full. Where we mention taste or mixability, we are summarizing what buyers widely report, not claiming a result of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each product had to clear five bars. Isolate had to be the primary protein source, so blends that print isolate in large type while listing concentrate first were out. The full formula had to be disclosed, with no proprietary blends hiding how much of the protein comes from what. The protein figure had to come from whey itself, with no added free amino acids such as glycine or taurine that can inflate a protein reading, a practice known as amino spiking. The product had to carry a recognized certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, or publish independent lab results. And the cost per 25 g of protein had to make sense for what you get.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also deliberately spread the list across needs rather than ranking six near-identical tubs. There is an everyday default, an option for people who want minimal additives, one for strict low-carb diets, two NSF-certified options for tested athletes, one of them unflavored for people who cook with their protein, and a value option. If none of the six fits, the selection criteria above work just as well for judging any isolate you find on a shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Protein per Scoop and Cost per 25 g
          </h2>

          <p>
            Comparing protein powders by tub price or serving price is how buyers get misled. Brands set their own scoop sizes, so a product with a 20 g scoop and one with a 28 g scoop can both advertise the same number of servings while delivering very different amounts of protein. The only fair comparison is cost per gram of protein, and 25 g is a convenient unit because it is close to a typical single serving and lands within the 20 to 40 g per-meal range that research supports.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calculation is simple. Divide the tub price by the number of servings, divide that by the grams of protein per serving, then multiply by 25. As a worked example with round numbers, a tub costing 60 dollars with 30 servings of 20 g works out to 2 dollars per serving and 2.50 dollars per 25 g of protein. A tub costing 70 dollars with 30 servings of 28 g works out to about 2.08 dollars per 25 g, which makes the more expensive tub the cheaper protein. Those numbers are illustrations only; run the sum on the prices you actually see, because they change often.
          </p>

